{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795808750,"nonce":"nR6I7lv4XRnSK4ndejwKak+MBnQ=","title":"Forgejo AGit-flow pull request may be treated as the repository's own code","message":"The Forgejo adapter computed a pull_request delivery's fork flag as head.repo.full_name != repository.full_name (crates/scarab-forge-forgejo/src/lib.rs, normalize). An AGit-flow pull request, created by pushing to refs/for/\u003cbranch\u003e (read access is enough), has its head in the base repository, so it came out as not a fork: not locked out of secrets, no fork limits. Reported by a reviewer on the 0.8.4 branch.\n\nFail-closed change made on fix/small-tickets-2026-11-28: a pull request is the repository's own code only when both repository names are present and equal AND head.ref names a branch (non-empty after dropping refs/heads/, not another refs/ ref). The dispatch check (dispatch_is_fork_code) applies the same head.ref rule.\n\nNot confirmed from the repository (no recorded AGit payload, no network used): what Forgejo actually sends in head.ref for an AGit pull request, in the webhook and in GET /repos/{o}/{r}/pulls/{n}. If it is refs/pull/\u003cn\u003e/head or a refs/for/... name, the change covers it. If it is a bare topic name that is not a branch, the webhook path still calls it the repository's code. A live check must: push to refs/for/main/topic on a test Forgejo with a read-only user, record the pull_request webhook body and the pulls/{n} answer (head.ref, head.repo, any flow field), add them as fixtures under crates/scarab-forge-forgejo/tests/fixtures, and, if head.ref is a bare name, add a check that the branch exists at head.sha (as dispatch does) or read the flow field.","files":null}]}